---
components: ["LayoutDirection"]
description: LayoutDirection sets the writing direction for part of a page, such as an Arabic or Hebrew form in an English app.
---

<script>
  import {
    Button,
    ClickableTile,
    Dropdown,
    LayoutDirection,
    Link,
    RangeSlider,
    Slider,
    Stack,
    Tab,
    TabContent,
    Tabs,
    Text,
    TextInput,
    TreeView,
  } from "carbon-components-svelte";
  import ArrowRight from "carbon-icons-svelte/lib/ArrowRight.svelte";
</script>

## Basic

Set `dir` to `"rtl"` or `"ltr"`. `LayoutDirection` renders a `div` with that `dir`, and components inside it read the direction from context. Use `tag` to render another element, such as `section` or `form`.

It matches Carbon React's `LayoutDirection`. To set the direction for the whole page, set `dir` on `<html>` instead.

<LayoutDirection dir="rtl" lang="ar" tag="form">
  <Stack gap={6}>
    <TextInput labelText="اسم الخدمة" placeholder="api-gateway" />
    <TextInput labelText="البريد الإلكتروني للمالك" placeholder="ops@example.com" />
    <Stack orientation="horizontal" gap={5}>
      <Button>نشر</Button>
      <Button kind="secondary">إلغاء</Button>
    </Stack>
  </Stack>
</LayoutDirection>

## Slider

In RTL, `Slider` and `RangeSlider` put `min` at the right end of the track. Dragging, clicking the track, marks, and the arrow keys follow it: <DocKbd label="ArrowLeft" /> raises the value.

A slider in a `LayoutDirection` follows its `dir` as it changes. Outside one, a slider reads the page direction when it mounts, and again when the pointer or keyboard focus reaches it.

<LayoutDirection dir="rtl" lang="ar">
  <Stack gap={7}>
    <Slider labelText="عدد النسخ" min={0} max={10} value={3} />
    <RangeSlider
      labelText="نطاق النسخ"
      min={0}
      max={10}
      value={2}
      valueUpper={6}
      marks={[
        { value: 0, label: "٠" },
        { value: 5, label: "٥" },
        { value: 10, label: "١٠" },
      ]}
    />
  </Stack>
</LayoutDirection>

## Tabs

Tabs run from right to left.

<LayoutDirection dir="rtl" lang="ar">
  <Tabs>
    <Tab label="نظرة عامة" />
    <Tab label="السجلات" />
    <Tab label="الإعدادات" />
    <svelte:fragment slot="content">
      <TabContent>حالة الخدمة وآخر عملية نشر.</TabContent>
      <TabContent>سجلات آخر ٢٤ ساعة.</TabContent>
      <TabContent>المنطقة وحدود الموارد.</TabContent>
    </svelte:fragment>
  </Tabs>
</LayoutDirection>

## TreeView

In RTL, rows indent from the right and a collapsed caret points left. <DocKbd label="ArrowLeft" /> expands a row or moves to its first child, and <DocKbd label="ArrowRight" /> collapses it or moves to its parent. Outside a `LayoutDirection`, the tree reads the page direction on each arrow key.

<LayoutDirection dir="rtl" lang="ar">
  <TreeView
    labelText="الخدمات"
    expandedIds={["analytics"]}
    nodes={[
      {
        id: "analytics",
        text: "التحليلات",
        nodes: [
          { id: "spark", text: "Apache Spark" },
          { id: "query", text: "استعلام SQL" },
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "storage",
        text: "التخزين",
        nodes: [{ id: "object-storage", text: "تخزين الكائنات" }],
      },
    ]}
  />
</LayoutDirection>

## Icons

In RTL, icons that point forward or back mirror: Pagination, PaginationNav, Carousel and Tabs arrows, Stepper and InterstitialScreen buttons, the AILabel undo icon, submenu carets and the ClickableTile icon. Icons without a direction stay as they are, such as check marks, the search icon and the Launch icon of an outbound link.

Add the `bx--icon--mirror-rtl` class to mirror your own forward or back icon, such as an arrow in a link's `icon` slot. A `dir="ltr"` region inside an RTL page turns the mirror off again.

<LayoutDirection dir="rtl" lang="ar">
  <Stack gap={5}>
    <ClickableTile href="#" icon={ArrowRight}>إعدادات الفوترة</ClickableTile>
    <Link href="#">
      عرض سجل النشر
      <ArrowRight slot="icon" class="bx--icon--mirror-rtl" />
    </Link>
  </Stack>
</LayoutDirection>

## Portalled menus

A menu or tooltip that renders in a portal leaves the subtree in the DOM, so it would lose the direction. `LayoutDirection` carries it to the portal. This covers `portalMenu` on Dropdown, ComboBox, and MultiSelect, a portalled DatePicker calendar, and every component that portals a tooltip or menu.

<LayoutDirection dir="rtl" lang="ar">
  <Dropdown
    portalMenu
    labelText="المنطقة"
    selectedId="0"
    items={[
      { id: "0", text: "شرق الولايات المتحدة" },
      { id: "1", text: "غرب أوروبا" },
      { id: "2", text: "جنوب شرق آسيا" },
    ]}
  />
</LayoutDirection>

## Mixed directions

Nest a `LayoutDirection` to switch back for one part, such as an English field in an RTL form. A `CodeSnippet` needs no wrapper: its code always reads left to right. See [CodeSnippet](/components/CodeSnippet#right-to-left).

<LayoutDirection dir="rtl" lang="ar">
  <Stack gap={5}>
    <Text>أدخل عنوان الخادم باللغة الإنجليزية:</Text>
    <LayoutDirection dir="ltr" lang="en">
      <TextInput labelText="Server URL" placeholder="https://api.example.com" />
    </LayoutDirection>
    <Text>نستخدم هذا العنوان في كل طلب.</Text>
  </Stack>
</LayoutDirection>

## Reading the direction

Call `getLayoutDirection` during component initialization to read the nearest direction in your own components. It returns a store that holds `"ltr"`, `"rtl"`, or `undefined` outside a `LayoutDirection`.

```svelte
<script>
  import { getLayoutDirection } from "carbon-components-svelte";

  const direction = getLayoutDirection();
</script>

<span>{$direction === "rtl" ? "←" : "→"}</span>
```
